<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kde domov můj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first performed 21 December <a href="/years/1834">1834</a>; the Czech part of the anthem of Czechoslovakia from <a href="/years/1918">1918</a>; anthem of the Czech Republic from <a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Kde domov můj&quot; began as one of twenty-one songs of incidental music that František Škroup composed for Josef Kajetán Tyl's comedy Fidlovačka aneb Žádný hněv a žádná rvačka (Fiddlers' Feast, or No Anger and No Brawl). Karel Strakatý first sang it at the Estates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 21 December 1834. The original song has two stanzas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kde-domov-muj.jpg" alt="Kde domov můj" width="330" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wiki-vr.mp · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kde-domov-můj-Škroup-Tyl-(1860).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song was written by Josef Kajetán Tyl for his comedy Fidlovačka and set to music by František Škroup. Tyl is said to have considered leaving it out of the play because he doubted its quality. It nevertheless soon became the most popular song of Fidlovačka, which gained popularity among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> who were seeking to revive their identity within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, and it quickly became their unofficial patriotic anthem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was formed in 1918, the first verse of the song became the Czech part of the national anthem, followed by the first stanza of the Slovak song Nad Tatrou sa blýska, with official translations into German and Hungarian. During the German-occupied Protectorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> from 1939 to 1945 the state kept the song as its anthem. It was usually played alongside the German anthems, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a> and the Nazi Horst-Wessel-Lied.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> split in December 1992, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> kept Kde domov můj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> kept Nad Tatrou sa blýska. Slovakia extended its anthem with a second verse, but the Czech anthem was adopted unextended, in its single-verse version, and its lyrics are fixed in Czech Act No. 3/1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The melody has been taken up by other composers. Johann Strauss the Younger included its motif in his Slaven-Potpourri of 1847, and in 1882 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Antonín Dvořák</a> used it in his overture to Šamberk's play Josef Kajetán Tyl, which is often performed alone as Domov můj, a concert work of some ten minutes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainer-maria-rilke">Rainer Maria Rilke</a> paid tribute to it in two poems in German, published in 1895.</p><p>Otakar Jeremiáš orchestrated the anthem in three versions, for large orchestra in 1930, for small orchestra in 1932 and for organ and orchestra in 1932, and they are still performed. In 2008 the Czech government released an official recording of the anthem by the National Theatre Opera Chorus and Orchestra, in four versions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kde domov můj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
